#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""t715-isa-audit: can these AArch64 ELF files run on an ARMv8.0-A CPU?

The SM-T715 (Exynos 5433: Cortex-A53 + Cortex-A57) implements ARMv8.0-A with
the optional features fp, asimd, aes, pmull, sha1, sha2 and crc32, nothing
newer. Code built for a later CPU dies there with SIGILL. This tool scans the
executable code of AArch64 ELF files (executables, shared objects, relocatable
objects, Arch packages) for instructions of later extensions.

    t715-isa-audit [--json] [--run] [--faults N] PATH...

PATH is an ELF file, a directory (walked, symlinks not followed) or an Arch
package (*.pkg.tar.zst/.xz/.gz, extracted with bsdtar or tar). Files that are
not 64-bit little-endian AArch64 ELF are skipped and counted in the summary.

Verdicts: OK (no fault-class instruction outside the outline-atomics helpers),
RISK (fault-class instructions found; counts and the first fault addresses are
printed), ERROR (unparseable ELF). Exit status: 0 when no file is RISK (and,
with --run, no program got SIGILL), 1 otherwise, 2 on usage error.

Limits: this is a static scan. Code that a library only runs after checking
the CPU (ifunc resolvers, getauxval(AT_HWCAP) dispatch, as in glibc, OpenSSL
or ONNX Runtime kernels) is counted like any other code, so RISK means "can
fault", not "will fault"; --run on the tablet gives the dynamic answer. Data
words inside executable sections can look like instructions; "$d" mapping
symbols are honoured when the symbol table has them.

Python 3 standard library only (runs on macOS and on the tablet).
"""

